  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
